# 3 readings by claude-opus-5-5, compared
aristotle-politics-4.md:1: argue/readings: S3 is read 3 ways: aristotle-politics-4.logic.r2 has "only_parts ∧ differ" and "only_parts ∧ differ → □combos_species"; aristotle-politics-4.logic.r2.2 has "only_parts ∧ differ → □combos_species"; they differ when only "these are the only parts of an animal" is true, which the second allows; aristotle-politics-4.logic.r2.3 has "□(only_parts ∧ differ → combos_species)" and "only_parts ∧ differ"
aristotle-politics-4.md:1: argue/answered: S3: answered no, which keeps aristotle-politics-4.logic.r2 and aristotle-politics-4.logic.r2.3, to: Suppose "these are the only parts of an animal" is true and "there are differences between these parts (in their sorts of stomachs, bellies, sensoriums and motive powers)", "necessarily, (if (these are the only parts of an animal and there are differences between these parts (in their sorts of stomachs, bellies, sensoriums and motive powers)), then the number of the combinations of all these parts makes up the different species of animals)", "necessarily, the number of the combinations of all these parts makes up the different species of animals" and "necessarily, the number of the combinations of all these parts makes up the different species of animals" are false. Is S3, "If, then, these are the only parts of an animal and there are differences between them; namely, in their various sorts of stomachs, bellies, and sensoriums: to which we must add their motive powers; the number of the combinations of all these must necessarily make up the different species of animals.", true then?
aristotle-politics-4.md:1: argue/readings: the conclusion is read 3 ways: aristotle-politics-4.logic.r2 has "□combos_species"; aristotle-politics-4.logic.r2.2 has "only_parts ∧ differ → □combos_species"; they differ when only "these are the only parts of an animal" is true, which the second allows; aristotle-politics-4.logic.r2.3 has "combos_species"
aristotle-politics-4.md:1: argue/modal-ambiguous: the conclusion is read aristotle-politics-4.logic.r2.3 with no modality; aristotle-politics-4.logic.r2 and aristotle-politics-4.logic.r2.2 as what is necessary or possible: its "must" does not say which it means
aristotle-politics-4.md:1: argue/answered: the conclusion: answered no, which keeps aristotle-politics-4.logic.r2, to: Suppose "these are the only parts of an animal" and "the number of the combinations of all these parts makes up the different species of animals" are true and "there are differences between these parts (in their sorts of stomachs, bellies, sensoriums and motive powers)", "necessarily, the number of the combinations of all these parts makes up the different species of animals" and "necessarily, the number of the combinations of all these parts makes up the different species of animals" are false. Is the conclusion, "If, then, these are the only parts of an animal and there are differences between them; namely, in their various sorts of stomachs, bellies, and sensoriums: to which we must add their motive powers; the number of the combinations of all these must necessarily make up the different species of animals.", true then?
